{
  "dom_id": "step-q134112",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "It's known that there is a linear correlation between the height (in $\\text{cm}$) and weight (in $\\text{kg}$) of a group of students in a particular class. Given that the correlation coefficient is $\\rho ≈0.45,$ which of the following scatter plots corresponds to this situation?",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that the linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\rho \\le 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho =1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation, and $\\rho =-1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation.\n\nTherefore, since $\\rho ≈0.45,$ there is a *weak positive* linear correlation in the data.\n\n- The *positive* linear correlation means that the data points should be scattered along a line with a *positive* slope.\n\n- Since the correlation is *weak*, most of the points should be scattered *far* from the trend line.\n\nTherefore, this situation corresponds to the following scatter plot.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14226558/assets/31aef47ad76d8cd4c7e967ccc7b8ca523eb3a20af440454bc7908bbe9441c918.png)",
